This news comes at a good time, given that I'm very close to a working
tiles version of NetHack 4 (the fanmade attempt to resume NetHack
development), and have been lamenting the issues with the default
tileset. NetHack 4 uses a few more tiles than 3.4.3, but only a very
small number.

NetHack 4's tiles system works by showing ASCII by default until a
player selects a tileset, so there is no "default tileset" in that
sense, but I'd love to add DawnHack to the default list of supported
tilesets when the tiles version is finally working.

One feature that might cause trouble, though, is NetHack 4's ability to
represent concepts like "upstairs behind a wand" (drawn, in the ASCII
port, via using a red background). Ideally, this would be represented
using the tile for the wand on top of the tile for the upstairs, but
this requires adding a 17th color to the tiles, "transparent". (The
current representation of this is internally in the game is to use the
color (71, 108, 108), or #476c6c, as a color key for "transparent",
using (72, 108, 108), or #486c6c, to represent the solid dark cyan
that's typically used for floor in the original NetHack tileset. This
is for compatibility with Slash'EM, which currently provides NetHack
4's default tileset). Sadly, though, this feature does not currently
work with the tiles port, because the existing 16x16 tiles do not have
a transparent background (and the 32x32 tiles that exist in Slash'EM
are unfinished and do not contain every tile). This feature is also
used for "brandings", small icons floating over tiles that indicate
things like "this door is locked", or "this monster is tame"; the
current brandings, I drew by myself, but I'm not much of an artist.

In other news, you might be relieved to hear that NetHack 4 has a fix in
place for the tiles-showing-as-other-tiles issues that plague 3.4.3:
tiles are identified by name, not number, in the text versions of the
tilesets. I haven't written the script to import tiles from images yet,
but given that you have a .txt version of the tileset already, hopefully
we can avoid that issue altogether

